25: etc .), with various combinations of multiple elements and layouts. Complex 20 content and layout, noisy data, font and style variations make automatic document understanding very 21 challenging. For example, to understand text-rich documents such as letters, a system needs to focus 22 almost exclusively on text content, paying attention to a long sequential context, while processing 23 semi-structured documents such as forms requires the system to analyze spatially distributed short 24 words, paying particular attention to the spatial arrangement of the words. Following the success 25 of BERT [4] on NLP tasks, there has been growing interest in developing pretraining methods 26 for document understanding [5, 6, 7, 8]. Pretrained models have achieved state-of-the-art (SoTA) 27 performance across diverse document understanding tasks [9, 10]. 28 Huge training datasets help pretraining models to learn a good representation for downstream tasks, 29 however, we observe three major problems with the current pretraining setup: (1) documents are 30 composed of semantic regions . Most of the recent document pretraining works follow BERT and split 31 documents into words. However, unlike the sequence-to-sequence learning in NLP, documents have a 32 hierarchical structure (words form sentences, sentences form a semantic region, and semantic regions 33 form a document). Also, the importance of words and sentences are highly context-dependent, i.e ., 34 the same word or sentence may have different importance in a different context. Moreover, current 35 transformer-based document pretraining models suffer from input length constraints. Also, input 36 Submitted to 35th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021). Do not distribute. length becomes a problem for text-rich documents or multi-page documents. (2) documents are 37 more than words . The semantic structure of the document is not only determined by the text within 38 it but also the visual features such as table, font size and style, and ﬁgure, etc . Moreover, the 39 visual appearance of the text within a block are often overlooked. Most of recent BERT-based pre- 40 training works only take the words as input without considering multimodal content and alignment 41 of multimodal information within semantic regions. (3) documents have spatial layout . Visual and 42 layout information is critical for document understanding. Recent works encode spatial information 43 via 2D position encoding and model spatial relationships with self-attention, which computes attention 44 weights for long inputs [5, 6]. However, for semi-structured documents, such as forms and receipts, 45 words are more related to their local surroundings. This corresponds strongly with human intuition – 46 when we look at magazines or newspapers, the receptive ﬁelds are modulated by our reading order 47 and attention. Based on the above observations, we ask the following question: can uniﬁed document 48 pretraining beneﬁt all of these different kinds of documents? 49 We propose a uniﬁed pretraining framework for document understanding, shown in Fig. 1. Our 50 model integrates image information in the pretraining stage by taking advantage of the transformer 51 architecture to learn cross-modal interactions between visual and textual information. To handle 52 textual information, we encode sentences using a hierarchical transformer encoder. The ﬁrst level 53 of the hierarchical encoder models the formation of the sentences from words. The second level 54 models the formation of the document from sentences. With the help of the hierarchical structure, 55 UniDoc learns how words form sentences and how sentences form documents. Meanwhile, it reduces 56 model computation complexity exponentially and increases the number of input words. This also 57 mimics human reading behaviors since the sentence/paragraph is a reasonable unit for people to 58 read and understand—people rarely check the interactions between arbitrary words across different 59 regions in order to understand an article. Convolution has been very successful in the extraction of 60 local features that encode visual and spatial information [11], so we use convolution layers as a more 61 efﬁcient complement to self-attention for addressing local intra-region dependencies in a document 62 image. Meanwhile, self-attention uses all input tokens to generate attention weights for capturing 63 global dependencies. Thus, we combine convolution with self-attention to form a mixed attention 64 mechanism that combines the advantages of the two operations. 65 We depart from previous vision-language pretraining [12, 13] by extracting both the textual and 66 visual features for each semantic region. We propose a novel gated cross-attentional transformer 67 that enables information exchange between modalities. A visually-rich region (ﬁgure, chart, etc ) 68 may have stronger visual information than textual information. Instead of treating outputs from both 69 modalities identically, we design a gating mechanism that can dynamically control the inﬂuence of 70 textual and visual features. This approach enables cross-modal connections and allows for variable 71 highlight the relevant information in visual and textual modality and enables cross-modal connections. 72 During pretraining, the CNN-based visual backbone and multi-layer gated cross-attention encoder 73 are jointly trained in both pretraining and ﬁne-tuning phase. 74 Our contributions are summarized as follows: (1) We introduce UniDoc, a powerful pretraining 75 framework for document understanding. UniDoc is capable of learning contextual textual and 76 visual information and cross-modal correlations within a single framework, which leads to better 77 performance. (2) We present Masked Sentence Modeling for language modeling, Visual Contrastive 78 Learning for vision modeling, and Vision-Language Alignment for pretraining. (3) We present 79 extensive experiments and analyses to validate the effectiveness of the proposed UniDoc. Extensive 80 experiments and analysis provide useful insights on the effectiveness of the pretraining tasks and 81 show outstanding performance on various downstream tasks. 82
29: 83 Self-supervised learning has shown great success in producing generic representations that learn 84 from large-scale unlabeled corpora [4]. Like the development of pretraining in computer vision [14] 85 and NLP [4], there has been a surging interest in self-supervised learning for Vision-Language 86 (VL) tasks [12, 15, 13, 16, 17, 18]. Transformers [4] are the key technology that enables learning 87 contextualized representations from large-scale unlabeled training data. These methods generally 88 employ BERT-like objectives to learn cross-modal representations from visual region features and 89 word embeddings. A major difference between natural images and document images is that documents 90 2 . trained during are designed by humans. Unlike the image-caption pairs used in VL, document images do not have 91 captions, and the visual and textual information are co-occur within semantic regions. 92 Several recent works have explored pretraining on document images [5, 6, 19, 20]. LayoutLM [5] 93 extends BERT to learn contextualized word representations for document images through multi-task 94 learning. It takes a sequence of OCR words as input during pretraining and incorporates the 2D 95 position embedding as input for each token. However, LayoutLM only considers textual information 96 during pretraining without modeling the alignment between visual and textual information–visual 97 information is only incorporated into the model during the ﬁne-tuning stage. The most recent 98 version, LayoutLMv2 [6], improves on this by incorporating the image encoder into pretraining and 99 jointly training the image encoder along with the BERT model. LayoutLMv2 splits the document 100 image into several parts and concatenates the visual embeddings and text embeddings into a single 101 sequence. Apart from masked token learning for language, LayoutLMv2 also considers image-text 102 alignment and image-text matching during pretraining. LAMPERT [8] proposes a structure that 103 exploits pretraining tasks to learn a hierarchical framework. It uses the English Wikipedia pages as the 104 target dataset, which provides the parsed content blocks. The most related work to ours is SelfDoc [7], 105 which proposes a multimodal document pretraining framework. It ﬁrst extracts the document object 106 proposals from pre-trained Faster R-CNN [21] and then applies OCR for each proposal to get the 107 words. It takes the pre-extracted RoI features and sentence embeddings as input, and models the 108 perform learning over the textual and visual information using the cross-modality encoder. 109 There is a noticeable difference between our proposed method, UniDoc, and other concurrent works 110 in document pretraining. Unlike the ﬁxed document object detector in [7], the parameters of the 111 image encoder with RoI align, which derive the visual features for semantic regions, are also updated 112 in UniDoc. In contrast to [6], our visual features come from the semantic regions instead of splitting 113 the image into ﬁxed regions. Moreover, to learn the contextualized visual representations, UniDoc 114 masks visual information in the latent space and learns contextualized representations by solving a 115 contrastive learning task deﬁned over a quantization of the latent visual embeddings. 116
35:3.1 Model Architecture
37: 118 Fig. 1 illustrates our approach, UniDoc, which consists of four components: feature extraction, feature 119 embedding, multi-layer gated cross-attention encoder, and pretraining tasks. Given a document image 120 and the locations of document elements (sentence or RoI), UniDoc takes image regions and words 121 that correspond to each document elements as inputs, and extracts their respective embeddings 122 through a visual feature extractor and a sentence encoder. These embeddings are then fed into a 123 transformer-based encoder to learn the cross-modal contextualized embeddings that integrate both 124 visual features and textual features. 125 3 In the feature extraction step, we ﬁrst employ an off-the-shelf OCR tool [22] to extract text from a doc- 126 ument image I , where the words are grouped into sentences S = { s 1 , . . . , s N } whose corresponding 127 bounding boxes are P = { p 1 , . . . , p N } . For each sentence bounding box p i , we use a ConvNet-based 128 backbone f ImEnc and RoI Align [23] f RoIAlign to extract the pooled RoI features v i . To obtain a feature 129 embedding , we extract the sentence embedding s i for each sentence s i via a pretrained sentence 130 encoder f SentEnc . Each region’s RoI feature v i is discretized into a ﬁnite set of visual representations 131 v Qi ∈ V Q via product quantization [24]. The multi-layer Gated Cross-Attention encoder takes the 132 position information, masked visual features ˜V and masked textual features ˜S as inputs, and then it 133 generates the contextualized multimodal representations ( H lV and H lS , l ∈ [1 , L ] ) and outputs the 134 predicted features ( ˆV and ˆS ), where L is the number of stacked transformer blocks. 135 More formally, the pretraining procedure can be decomposed into the following steps: 136 where f Mask denotes the masking function that randomly masks RoI features and sentence embeddings 137 with the respective probabilities p v Mask and p s Mask . L Pretraining is composed of three pretraining tasks: 138 Masked Sentence Modeling (MSM), Visual Contrastive Learning (VCL), and Vision-Language 139 Alignment (VLA). Next, we provide details mentioned in Eq. 1. 140 Feature Extraction and Embedding. Formally, a document image I ∈ R W × H consists 141 of N regions, where each region’s bounding box is characterized by a 6-d vector, as p i = 142 { x LT W , y LT H , x RB W , y RB H , wW , hH } , where w and h are of the width and height the region, W and H are the 143 width and height of I , while ( x LT , y LT ) and ( x RB , y RB ) denote the coordinates of the top-left and 144 bottom-right corners respectively. The 6-d vector is mapped into a high-dimensional representation 145 via a linear mapping function. 146 The visual embedding is the sum of the mapped RoI feature and position embedding. Likewise, the 147 textual embedding is the sum of sentence embedding and position embedding. We also have different 148 types of segments to distinguish different modalities. The input sequence to the transformer-based 149 encoder starts with a special start element ([CLS] and full visual features), then it is followed by 150 multimodal elements, and it ends with a special ending element ([SEP]+full visual features). For 151 the special elements ([CLS] and [SEP]), the corresponding full visual features are features extracted 152 from the whole input image, by applying f ImEnc to an RoI covering the whole input image. 153 Quantization Module. Unlike the ﬁxed image encoder in [7], we jointly learn the image encoder 154 in an end-to-end fashion alongside the multimodal model. A visual representation can be learned 155 by predicting the visual features of the masked regions, but it is challenging to predict such features 156 exactly, since they are unconstrained and of continuous representation. To constrain the representation 157 space of the visual features and facilitate the end-to-end learning of image encoder (see Task #2 158 in Sec. 3.2) , we follow [25, 26] and use vector quantization to discretize the visual features V = 159 { v 1 , . . . , v N } into a ﬁnite set of representations V Q = { v Q 1 , . . . , v QN } . Speciﬁcally, we deﬁne latent 160 embedding spaces e ∈ R C × E , where C is the number of codebooks, and E is the number of entries 161 for each codebook. For each v i , we ﬁrst map it to logits v (cid:96)i ∈ R C × E , and calculate the probability 162 for the j -th codebook entry in i -th group as p c,e = exp (( v (cid:96)c,e + g e ) /τ ) / (cid:80) Ek =1 exp (( v (cid:96)c,k + g k ) /τ ) , 163 where τ is a non-negative temperature, g 1: E are i.i.d samples drawn from Gumbel(0,1) distribution. 164 During the forward pass, we choose one entry vector from each codebook by ˜ e i ∼ argmax e p c,e 165 and generate the quantized representation v Qi by a concatenation of { ˜ e 1 , . . . , ˜ e G } which is then 166 followed by a linear transformation. During the backward pass, the gradients are computed through a 167 Gumbel-Softmax estimator [27]. 168 Gated Cross-Attention. To model the interactions among multimodal inputs, we introduce a 169 multimodal transformer with gated cross-attention to model the cross-modality relationships. Let 170 H l +1 m be output features at the l -th layer for one modality m , and let n be another modality ( m, n ∈ 171 { V, S } ). We obtain the features at ( l + 1) -th layer as: 172 where f LN denotes layer normalization [28]. The feed-forward sub- layer f FF in Eq. 2 is further 173 composed of two fully-connected sub-layers, both wrapped in residual adds and f LN . 174 4 The core part of Eq. 2 is the cross-attention f Cross-Att ( · ) . Given the intermediate representations H lm 175 and H ln , the cross-attention output for modality m is computed as: 176 where f iq ( H lm ) , f ik ( H ln ) , and f iv ( H ln ) are the query , key , and value calculated by linear mapping 177 layers for the i -th head. d is the model dimension, h is the number of heads, and U is the weight 178 matrix that combines the outputs of the heads. 179 Considering the substantial diversity of document images and the different information needs of 180 differing document types, we use a gating mechanism [29] to dynamically weight the outputs of the 181 visual and textual branches. Speciﬁcally, we feed the concatenated the visual and textual features to a 182 non-linear network f Gate ([ H l +1 m ; H l +1 n ]) , which generates the modality-speciﬁc attention weights α lm 183 and α ln , and returns the weights separately to their respective modality-speciﬁc branches to perform 184 element-wise products. We multiply the features for modality m with its modality-speciﬁc attention 185 weight, and compute the updated feature as: H l +1 m = H l +1 m (1 + α lm ) , same that for modality n . 186
41: 187 The full pretraining objective of UniDoc (right block in Fig. 1) is deﬁned as: L Pretraining = L MSM + 188 L VCL + L VLA . In the rest of this section, we describe each task in detail. 189 Task #1 : Masked Sentence Modeling. This task is similar to the Masked Token Modeling task 190 utilized in BERT. The key difference is that we mask sentences instead of tokens. During pretraining, 191 each sentence and RoI of the input document is randomly and independently masked. For the masked 192 sentence, its token is replaced with a special sentence of [MASK]. The model is trained to predict 193 the masked sentence feature, based on the unmasked words and the visual features. The goal is to 194 predict the masked sentence embeddings based on the contextual information from the surrounding 195 sentences and image regions, by minimizing the smooth L1 loss [21]: 196 where Θ is the trainable parameters and f UniDoc ( . ) outputs the unmasked textual feature, s \ i is the 197 surrounding features for the i -th input, ˜V are the image features with random masking. 198 Task #2 : Visual Contrastive Learning. We learn visual feature representations by solving a visual 199 contrastive learning task which requires estimating the true quantized latent RoI representation. Given 200 a prediction ˆ v i ∈ ˆ V for the masked RoI ˜ v i ∈ ˜V , the model needs to estimate the positive quantized 201 representation v Qi in a set of quantized candidate representations V Q . Good representations are 202 learned by maximizing the agreement between output representation and quantized representation of 203 the same RoIs as follows: 204 where sim ( · , · ) computes the cosine similarity between two vectors, λ is a hyperparameter, and κ is a 205 temperature scalar. The second term encourages the model to use the codebook entries more equally. 206 Task #3 : Vision-Language Alignment. To enforce the alignment among different modalities, we 207 explicitly encourage alignment between words and image regions via similarity-preserving knowledge 208 distillation [30]. Note that, unlike the text-image alignment in LayoutLMv2 [6] which splits the 209 image into four regions and predicts whether the given word is covered or not on the image side, 210 we align the image and text belonging to the same region. The goal is to minimize the differences 211 between the pairwise similarities of sentence embeddings and the pairwise similarities of image 212 region features: 213 where S is the unmasked input sentence embeddings, H LV is the mapped visual representations of the 214 ﬁnal layer, || · || F is the Frobenius norm, and f Norm performs L2 normalization. 215 5
45: Pretraining corpus. We build our pretraining corpus based on IIT-CDIP Test Collection 1.0 [31], 218 which contains more than 11M scanned document images. To differentiate pretraining from ﬁnetuning, 219 we ﬁlter out the document images of RVL-CDIP [10] from IIT-CDIP since it is a subset of IIT-CDIP, 220 and sample 1M document images as our pretraining corpus. 221 Table 1 shows the dataset statistics. IIT-CDIP 222 only provides the OCR texts in XML format. 223 We extract words and their locations by applying 224 EasyOCR [22] on document images. As shown 225 in Fig. 2 (a), EasyOCR provides two kinds of 226 output modes: non-paragraph and paragraph. 227 The paragraph mode groups the non-paragraph 228 results into text regions. We adopt the paragraph- 229 mode outputs as the basic input elements since 230 text regions provide semantically more meaning- 231 ful information than independent words. There 232 are some advantages to such a design: (1) the 233 region-level design hierarchically encodes document elements and this facilitates the modeling 234 of latent relationships at the region level which has higher-level semantics than the word level. 235 (2) the hierarchical encoding also overcomes the input size limitation of word-level BERT-based 236 models [5, 6]. 237 Pretraining setting. We initialize the sentence encoder f SentEnc with BERT-NLI-STSb-base [34] 238 pretrained for NLI [35] and STS-B [36]. The ResNet-50 backbone in the image encoder is pretrained 239 on the PubLayNet training set [33]. All the parameters (except f SentEnc and f ImEnc ) are randomly 240 initialized. During pretraining, we freeze the parameters of f SentEnc and jointly train the visual 241 encoder and multi-modal UniDoc model in an end-to-end fashion. Such an end-to-end training 242 allows the ConvNet and Transformer to realize their full potentials in spatial and sequence modeling 243 for pretraining. UniDoc contains 12 layers of gated cross-attention transformer blocks. We set the 244 hidden size to 768 and the number of heads to 12, the maximum number of regions N to 64, and the 245 maximum input sequence length for f SentEnc to 512. The pretraining is conducted on 8 NVIDIA Tesla 246 V100 32GB GPUs with a batch size of 64. It is trained with Adam optimizer [37], with an initial 247 learning rate of 10 − 5 , weight decay of 10 − 4 , and learning rate warmup in the ﬁrst 20% iterations. 248 To learn a useful multimodal representation, random masking is applied to both textual and visual 249 inputs. For MSM, we set the mask probability p s Mask for input sentences to 15%. 80% among the 250 masked sentences are replaced by special sentence [CLS, MASK, SEP], while 10% sentences are 251 replaced by random sentences sampled from other documents, and 10% remains unchanged. For 252 VCL, the λ is set to 0.1, κ is set to 0.1, the mask probability p v Mask is set to 7.5% and the masked RoI 253 features are ﬁlled with zeros. The temperature τ is annealed from 2.0 to 0.5 by a factor of of 0.999995 254 at every iteration. We select the pretraining checkpoint with the lowest L Pretraining for ﬁnetuning stage. 255
53: 288 The importance of multimodal learning. To study the effect of multimodal learning, we experiment 289 in three different settings (1) Vision only (V): this setting omits the textual components of UniDoc 290 and adopts multilayer self-attention transformer to learn the visual representation. (2) Language 291 only (L): this setting omits the visual encoder and keeps only the textual components. (3) Vision- 292 Language (V+L): this setting considers both vision and language information. We ﬁrst train three 293 settings without pretraining. Table 2 shows consistent improvement across tasks for V+L over the 294 single-stream baselines (V or L). This demonstrates that our UniDoc model is able to learn important 295 visual-linguistic relationships that beneﬁt downstream tasks even without pretraining. 296 In Table 2, we ﬁnd that visual information dominates the performance of document classiﬁcation, 297 while language information contributes a lot to form understanding and receipt understanding. The 298 results also indicate that different document tasks rely on different information. For document entity 299 recognition tasks, language information is more important than visual features. As can be seen in 300 Fig. 2 (b) and (c), entity recognition is more word-oriented. On the other hand, document classiﬁcation 301 is more focused on global-level understanding. As a result, visual and layout information contribute 302 a lot to the ﬁnal prediction of the document classiﬁcation model. This matches well with the innate 303 abilities of humans to distinguish between document types without fully understanding the words. 304 Effect of pretraining tasks. We analyze the effectiveness of different pretraining settings through ab- 305 lation studies over FUNSD, CORD, and RVL-CDIP, which are representative document benchmarks. 306 Table 2 ablates the key design choices in pretraining UniDoc. For experimental efﬁciency, UniDoc 307 7 models evaluated here are trained with 5 epochs on 300k training corpus. Overall, the pretraining of 308 UniDoc consistently improves the performance over all three downstream tasks. The improvement 309 gains vary among different tasks. 310 We ﬁrst establish two baselines: MSM+MVM in Table 2 indicates the combination of masked sen- 311 tence learning and masked visual feature prediction. Similar to MSM, for MVM, we freeze the visual 312 backbone and perform masked visual feature prediction via RoI-feature regression. MSM+VCL 313 jointly trains the visual backbone end-to-end with contrastive learning. As shown in the Table 2, 314 MSM+MVM achieves better results than the model without pretraining. Furthermore, when combin- 315 ing VCL together with MSM, consistent performance gains are observed across all the benchmarks. 316 Among the three ﬁnetuning tasks, the improvements on FUNSD and CORD are bigger than on 317 RVL-CDIP. We think the local context modeling capability of the ConvNet-based image encoder 318 brings more beneﬁts to entity recognition, since entities are heavily linked and correlated to their 319 local surroundings. When MSM, VCL, and VLA are jointly trained, we observe further performance 320 gains across all the benchmarks. We also consider the image-text matching task (Rel) [15, 6], and 321 combine Rel with MSM+VCL+VLA. It hurts the performance of all three downstream tasks. We 322 conjecture that the image-text matching task introduces mismatched pairs of image and OCR texts as 323 negative examples that potentially hamper the training of other tasks. 324 Performance Comparison with SoTA. We further pretrain UniDoc on 1M document images with 325 5 epochs and report the ﬁnetuning results in Table 3. UniDoc outperforms previous models on 326 FUNSD and CORD, by a signiﬁcantly large margin, demonstrating that our proposed approach is 327 highly effective, partially due to the end-to-end training of the image encoder that improves the 328 semantic alignments between images and texts. Note that UniDoc is pretrained on a subset of 329 IIT-CDIP (1M document images), which is considerably less than the 11M document images used in 330 LayoutLM [5] and LayoutLMv2 [6]. TILT [39] builds a 1.1M pretraining corpus by combining RVL- 331 CDIP, UCSF Industry Documents Library, and Common Crawl. UniDoc also achieves promising 332 results on document classiﬁcation. Note that both LayoutLM v2 and TILT use Microsoft OCR, which 333 is a commercial service with a stronger OCR performance than EasyOCR, which is used in our 334 experiments. We ﬁnd that OCR plays a key role in document classiﬁcation performance. As shown 335 in Fig. 3, UniDoc performs the best on the ‘ email ’ category but worst on the ‘ form ’ category. We also 336 compare the results of different OCR engines: EasyOCR [22] vs. Tesseract [40]. The results show 337 that UniDoc with EasyOCR achieves a better performance than with Tesseract. EasyOCR is powered 338 by an advanced neural network, while Tesseract is based on less sophisticated techniques. 339 8 Effect of visual backbone. Addition- 340 ally, we apply the trained visual back- 341 bone to document object detection 342 on PubLayNet. The performance of 343 the F-RCNN on the validation set is 344 depicted in Table 4. To better com- 345 pare, we establish two F-RCNN mod- 346 els with: (1) backbone initialized with 347 ResNet-50 pretrained on ImageNet; (2) backbone initialized from UniDoc’s pretrained visual back- 348 bone. It can be seen that our pretrained backbone outperforms ImageNet-pretrained backbones. 349 By leveraging UniDoc, we can train different variants of the visual backbone and apply them to 350 document-speciﬁc downstream applications, without relying on incompatible pretrained backbones 351 from other domains ( e.g ., natural image). Moreover, the visual backbone of UniDoc does not require 352 any custom layers, and thus any ConvNet architecture can be used in place of ResNet. 353
57: 354 We develop UniDoc, a uniﬁed pretraining framework for document understanding. Our model 355 introduces a novel joint training framework that effectively exploits the visual and textual information 356 during pretraining and ﬁnetuning. We evaluate the UniDoc comprehensively on three downstream 357 tasks: form understanding, receipt understanding, and document image classiﬁcation. Extensive 358 empirical analysis demonstrates that the pretraining procedure can take advantage of multimodal 359 inputs and effectively aggregating and aligning visual and textual information of document images 360 with the proxy tasks. This work has a broader impact on document applications. By ﬁnetuning the 361 pretrained UniDoc on task-speciﬁc data, document processing systems can provide better results and 362 reduce the expensive data annotations costs. In terms of negative social impact, the document images 363 used for pretraining may contain sensitive information and therefore the models trained on such data 364 may inappropriately leak some private information. To address the privacy leakage, it is worthwhile 365 to explore the combination of privacy-preserving learning and self-supervised learning. 366 There are interesting short- and long-term research directions for UniDoc: (1) we freeze the sentence 367 encoder during pretraining and ﬁne-tuning phases due to computational constraints. A better doc- 368 ument representation can be learned by jointly training the sentence encoder, visual backbone and 369 cross-attention encoder in a completely end-to-end fashion. (2) Although impressive performance 370 has been achieved in document entity recognition tasks such as form and receipt understanding, the 371 classiﬁcation accuracy on semi-structured documents such as forms is still inferior to that of rich-text 372 documents. It is possible to devise a better method to model the spatial relationship among words. 373 (3) An interesting direction is to extend UniDoc to multipage/multilingual document pretraining. 374 Additionally, there exist many text-based labeled document datasets in the NLP domain, such as 375 document summarization. Can we transfer the knowledge learned from the text-based document 376 domain to the image-based document domain? Lastly, in addition to scanned documents, using digital 377 PDF as part of the pretraining data is a direction worth exploring since it provides rich metadata 378 which could be beneﬁcial for multimodal learning. 379 9
